DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The _c_p_u___s_e_t___t data structure represents a set of CPUs.   CPU  sets  are
       used by sscchheedd__sseettaaffffiinniittyy(2) and similar interfaces.

       The  _c_p_u___s_e_t___t  data  type  is implemented as a bit mask.  However, the
       data structure should be treated as opaque:  all  manipulation  of  CPU
       sets should be done via the macros described in this page.

       The following macros are provided to operate on the CPU set _s_e_t:

       CCPPUU__ZZEERROO()
              Clears _s_e_t, so that it contains no CPUs.

       CCPPUU__SSEETT()
              Add CPU _c_p_u to _s_e_t.

       CCPPUU__CCLLRR()
              Remove CPU _c_p_u from _s_e_t.

       CCPPUU__IISSSSEETT()
              Test to see if CPU _c_p_u is a member of _s_e_t.

       CCPPUU__CCOOUUNNTT()
              Return the number of CPUs in _s_e_t.

       Where  a _c_p_u argument is specified, it should not produce side effects,
       since the above macros may evaluate the argument more than once.

       The first CPU on the system corresponds to a _c_p_u value of 0,  the  next
       CPU  corresponds to a _c_p_u value of 1, and so on.  No assumptions should
       be made about particular CPUs being available, or the set of CPUs being
       contiguous, since CPUs can be taken offline dynamically or be otherwise
       absent.  The constant CCPPUU__SSEETTSSIIZZEE (currently 1024)  specifies  a  value
       one  greater  than  the  maximum  CPU  number  that  can  be  stored in
       _c_p_u___s_e_t___t.

       The following macros perform logical operations on CPU sets:

       CCPPUU__AANNDD()
              Store the intersection of the sets _s_r_c_s_e_t_1 and _s_r_c_s_e_t_2 in  _d_e_s_t_‐
              _s_e_t (which may be one of the source sets).

       CCPPUU__OORR()
              Store  the  union  of  the  sets  _s_r_c_s_e_t_1 and _s_r_c_s_e_t_2 in _d_e_s_t_s_e_t
              (which may be one of the source sets).

       CCPPUU__XXOORR()
              Store the XOR of the sets _s_r_c_s_e_t_1 and _s_r_c_s_e_t_2 in _d_e_s_t_s_e_t  (which
              may  be  one of the source sets).  The XOR means the set of CPUs
              that are in either _s_r_c_s_e_t_1 or _s_r_c_s_e_t_2, but not both.

       CCPPUU__EEQQUUAALL()
              Test whether two CPU set contain exactly the same CPUs.

