       Because some applications may require the ability to  dynamically  size
       CPU  sets (e.g., to allocate sets larger than that defined by the stan‐
       dard _c_p_u___s_e_t___t data type), glibc nowadays provides a set of  macros  to
       support this.

       The following macros are used to allocate and deallocate CPU sets:

       CCPPUU__AALLLLOOCC()
              Allocate  a  CPU set large enough to hold CPUs in the range 0 to
              _n_u_m___c_p_u_s_-_1.

       CCPPUU__AALLLLOOCC__SSIIZZEE()
              Return the size in bytes of the CPU set that would be needed  to
              hold CPUs in the range 0 to _n_u_m___c_p_u_s_-_1.  This macro provides the
              value that can be used for the _s_e_t_s_i_z_e argument in the CCPPUU__**__SS()
              macros described below.

       CCPPUU__FFRREEEE()
              Free a CPU set previously allocated by CCPPUU__AALLLLOOCC().

       The  macros  whose names end with "_S" are the analogs of the similarly
       named macros without the suffix.  These macros perform the  same  tasks
       as  their  analogs, but operate on the dynamically allocated CPU set(s)
       whose size is _s_e_t_s_i_z_e bytes.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       CCPPUU__IISSSSEETT() and CCPPUU__IISSSSEETT__SS() return nonzero if _c_p_u is in  _s_e_t;  other‐
       wise, it returns 0.

       CCPPUU__CCOOUUNNTT() and CCPPUU__CCOOUUNNTT__SS() return the number of CPUs in _s_e_t.

       CCPPUU__EEQQUUAALL()  and  CCPPUU__EEQQUUAALL__SS()  return nonzero if the two CPU sets are
       equal; otherwise, they return 0.

       CCPPUU__AALLLLOOCC() returns a pointer on success, or NULL on failure.   (Errors
       are as for mmaalllloocc(3).)

       CCPPUU__AALLLLOOCC__SSIIZZEE()  returns  the  number of bytes required to store a CPU
       set of the specified cardinality.

       The other functions do not return a value.

